1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,065
Your build works. The jar sits on your laptop. And your teammate cannot use it.

2
00:00:05,615 --> 00:00:06,940
Until you publish it,

3
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:09,715
to the repository your whole company shares.

4
00:00:09,715 --> 00:00:11,418
That step is called deploy.

5
00:00:11,968 --> 00:00:14,348
Today you learn the two kinds of version,

6
00:00:14,348 --> 00:00:15,567
snapshot and release,

7
00:00:15,567 --> 00:00:18,005
and the one rule that surprises everybody.

8
00:00:18,555 --> 00:00:21,094
Then you publish, and break the rule on purpose.

9
00:00:21,644 --> 00:00:22,764
One honest note.

10
00:00:22,764 --> 00:00:27,804
The company repository in this lesson is a small local server I started,

11
00:00:27,804 --> 00:00:30,184
that accepts uploads with a login.

12
00:00:30,184 --> 00:00:34,734
The real thing is a repository manager like nexus or artifactory,

13
00:00:34,734 --> 00:00:37,324
but Maven uploads to it the same way.

14
00:00:38,874 --> 00:00:42,654
Install copies your jar into your own dot m two folder.

15
00:00:42,654 --> 00:00:45,471
Deploy uploads it to a remote repository,

16
00:00:45,471 --> 00:00:47,258
so others can download it.

17
00:00:47,808 --> 00:00:51,415
A repository manager usually has two repositories.

18
00:00:51,415 --> 00:00:52,714
One for snapshots,

19
00:00:52,714 --> 00:00:54,229
and one for releases.

20
00:00:54,779 --> 00:00:56,647
A snapshot is work in progress.

21
00:00:56,647 --> 00:00:58,815
The version ends with dash snapshot.

22
00:00:58,815 --> 00:01:00,923
You may publish it again and again.

23
00:01:01,473 --> 00:01:06,913
A release is final. The version has no snapshot. Once published, it can never be replaced.

24
00:01:07,463 --> 00:01:09,308
The pom says where to upload.

25
00:01:09,308 --> 00:01:11,662
In the block distribution management,

26
00:01:11,662 --> 00:01:13,317
one address for snapshots,

27
00:01:13,317 --> 00:01:14,653
and one for releases.

28
00:01:16,703 --> 00:01:20,522
Why can a release never be replaced? Because other people built on it.

29
00:01:21,072 --> 00:01:24,458
If the jar for one dot zero dot zero changed tomorrow,

30
00:01:24,458 --> 00:01:26,653
an old build could not be repeated.

31
00:01:26,653 --> 00:01:29,349
The same version would mean different code.

32
00:01:29,899 --> 00:01:31,760
So the repository refuses.

33
00:01:31,760 --> 00:01:33,477
It answers four oh nine,

34
00:01:33,477 --> 00:01:34,122
conflict.

35
00:01:34,122 --> 00:01:36,698
The right fix is never to overwrite.

36
00:01:36,698 --> 00:01:38,774
Publish one dot zero dot one.

37
00:01:39,324 --> 00:01:40,956
A snapshot is different.

38
00:01:40,956 --> 00:01:42,588
Maven adds a time stamp,

39
00:01:42,588 --> 00:01:44,561
so each upload is a new file,

40
00:01:44,561 --> 00:01:46,193
and the newest one wins.

41
00:01:46,743 --> 00:01:47,639
And the login?

42
00:01:47,639 --> 00:01:49,175
The same as last lesson.

43
00:01:49,175 --> 00:01:50,135
A server block,

44
00:01:50,135 --> 00:01:51,223
with the same id,

45
00:01:51,223 --> 00:01:54,167
and the password from an environment variable.

46
00:01:56,217 --> 00:01:59,374
Go to the demo project. Type: CD, demo.

47
00:01:59,924 --> 00:02:03,074
Look at the upload addresses in the pom.

48
00:02:03,074 --> 00:02:03,861
Type: sed,

49
00:02:03,861 --> 00:02:04,412
dash n,

50
00:02:04,412 --> 00:02:06,302
eleven comma twenty-two,

51
00:02:06,302 --> 00:02:06,459
p,

52
00:02:06,459 --> 00:02:07,562
pom dot X M L.

53
00:02:08,112 --> 00:02:14,298
Two repositories, snapshots and releases. Both with the id corp, so one login serves both.

54
00:02:14,848 --> 00:02:15,938
Set the password from the locked file.

55
00:02:15,938 --> 00:02:16,999
Type: export REPO_PASS equals dollar,

56
00:02:16,999 --> 00:02:17,343
parenthesis,

57
00:02:17,343 --> 00:02:17,458
cat,

58
00:02:17,458 --> 00:02:17,630
tilde,

59
00:02:17,630 --> 00:02:17,802
slash,

60
00:02:17,802 --> 00:02:18,347
dot corp dash pass.

61
00:02:18,897 --> 00:02:24,636
Deploy the snapshot. Type: mvn dash B dash q deploy, then list the snapshot folder.

62
00:02:25,586 --> 00:02:27,399
Maven builds, then uploads.

63
00:02:34,949 --> 00:02:37,338
One jar, with a time stamp in its name.

64
00:02:37,888 --> 00:02:42,112
Deploy it again. The same command.

65
00:02:44,562 --> 00:02:45,757
A second upload.

66
00:02:52,307 --> 00:02:56,616
Two jars now. Each upload is a new file, and nothing was refused.

67
00:02:57,166 --> 00:03:02,820
Now make it a release. Remove the word snapshot from the version, with sed.

68
00:03:03,370 --> 00:03:06,698
Version one dot zero dot zero. No snapshot.

69
00:03:07,248 --> 00:03:11,408
Deploy it. Type: mvn dash B dash q deploy, then list the release folder.

70
00:03:13,858 --> 00:03:15,052
Uploading the release.

71
00:03:21,602 --> 00:03:23,010
The release is published.

72
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:28,766
Now the rule. Deploy the same version again, and keep only the error.

73
00:03:29,316 --> 00:03:31,129
Same version, second time.

74
00:03:37,679 --> 00:03:38,558
Four oh nine.

75
00:03:38,558 --> 00:03:39,167
Conflict.

76
00:03:39,167 --> 00:03:41,601
The repository protects the release.

77
00:03:41,601 --> 00:03:42,886
To change anything,

78
00:03:42,886 --> 00:03:44,442
you need a new version.

79
00:03:45,992 --> 00:03:46,938
Your lab task.

80
00:03:46,938 --> 00:03:51,869
Lab ninety-one gives you a project and a repository that accepts uploads.

81
00:03:51,869 --> 00:03:53,693
Lab check judges your work.

82
00:03:54,243 --> 00:03:56,430
It lives at git hub dot com,

83
00:03:56,430 --> 00:03:57,992
slash it dash waves,

84
00:03:57,992 --> 00:03:59,788
slash dev ops dash A I,

85
00:03:59,788 --> 00:04:00,647
in academy,

86
00:04:00,647 --> 00:04:00,959
lab,

87
00:04:00,959 --> 00:04:02,755
lab ninety-one dot S H.

88
00:04:03,305 --> 00:04:06,857
One: set the variable corp pass from the password file,

89
00:04:06,857 --> 00:04:08,407
and deploy the snapshot,

90
00:04:08,407 --> 00:04:10,409
with dash s settings dot X M L.

91
00:04:10,959 --> 00:04:15,482
Two: change the version to one dot zero dot zero, and deploy the release.

92
00:04:16,032 --> 00:04:21,365
Three: deploy it again. It must fail. Save the error line into conflict dot text.

93
00:04:21,915 --> 00:04:25,969
Four: change the version to one dot zero dot one, and deploy that.

94
00:04:26,519 --> 00:04:28,862
Five: one sentence in why dot text,

95
00:04:28,862 --> 00:04:31,875
about why a release is never published twice.

96
00:04:31,875 --> 00:04:33,281
Use the word release.

97
00:04:33,831 --> 00:04:35,012
Install a J D K,

98
00:04:35,012 --> 00:04:37,151
Maven and python three first.

99
00:04:37,151 --> 00:04:38,037
Then lab up,

100
00:04:38,037 --> 00:04:38,774
lab check,

101
00:04:38,774 --> 00:04:39,734
and lab down.

102
00:04:39,734 --> 00:04:42,832
The shortcut file is shortcuts ninety-one.

103
00:04:42,832 --> 00:04:45,267
The check downloads plugins once,

104
00:04:45,267 --> 00:04:46,226
so be online.

105
00:04:48,276 --> 00:04:50,345
Pass or fail. You need all six.

106
00:04:50,895 --> 00:04:52,709
Five pass lines in lab check.

107
00:04:53,259 --> 00:04:55,243
You can explain snapshot and release.

108
00:04:55,793 --> 00:04:58,630
You can explain why the second release deploy fails.

109
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:01,655
The fix is a new version, never an overwrite.

110
00:05:02,205 --> 00:05:04,167
The password stays out of every file.

111
00:05:04,717 --> 00:05:06,637
lab down, nothing left behind.

112
00:05:07,187 --> 00:05:08,489
To pass, all six.

113
